      <div class="research-content">
        <p class="research-number">01</p>

        <h2>Machine Learning and Scientific Understanding</h2>

        <p>
          The first area of my research investigates whether machine-learning systems can contribute
          to scientific understanding even when they do not provide explanations in
          the traditional sense. One possibility is a dependency-based form of
          objectual understanding: by detecting complex statistical relationships,
          or by showing that an apparently relevant variable makes no difference to
          predictive performance, a machine-learning model can refine a scientist's
          mental model of a phenomenon. Statistical independencies may rule out
          certain previously plausible dependencies or causal structures, making the
          resulting model of the phenomenon more comprehensive. However, they do not
          by themselves explain why a dependency holds and they do not reveal causal
          relationships or underlying mechanisms. Their epistemic value
          therefore often lies in constraining the space of possible explanations
          and generating hypotheses for further theoretical or experimental
          investigation.
        </p>

        <p>
          I also examine whether explaining a successful predictive
          model can produce understanding of the real-world phenomenon it targets.
          Such a transfer is possible only when there is a justified connection
          between model and world. Scientists must determine what the model's inputs,
          outputs, internal representations, and learned dependencies correspond to
          in the target domain, and whether its predictions rely on scientifically
          meaningful features rather than accidental proxies. Explanations of the
          model may then reveal possible structures or processes in the phenomenon,
          but their significance depends on interpretive
          assumptions and the strength of the representational link between the
          model and its target. Understanding therefore obviously arises from interpreting
          a machine-learning model within its scientific context, rather than from
          analysing it in isolation.
        </p>
      </div>

    </article>

    <article class="research-area research-area-reversed">

      <div class="research-image">
  <img
