    <div class="research-content">
      <p class="research-number">03</p>

      <h2>Machine Learning and Objectivity in Climate Models</h2>

      <p>
        A third area of my research concerns the epistemological and normative
        consequences of integrating machine learning into scientific models, 
        especially climate models. The automation of scientific practices is sometimes assumed
        to reduce subjectivity by replacing expert judgement with data-driven
        optimisation. Yet machine learning does not eliminate methodological
        choices, but it relocates them to less visible stages of
        the modelling process, including data selection, model architecture,
        hyperparameter settings, loss functions, validation metrics, and
        decisions about how a machine-learning component is integrated into a
        larger scientific model. I investigate how these technical decisions
        express different epistemic priorities and how they affect the objectivity of the results.
      </p>

      <p>
        Climate modelling provides a central case study for this research because
        machine learning is increasingly used to emulate complex physical
        processes and replace traditional parameterisations that depend
        on expert judgement. In this context, scientists must negotiate
        trade-offs between predictive accuracy, interpretability, physical
        plausibility, robustness, computational efficiency, and sensitivity to
        uncertainty. These choices may also involve inductive risk, since
        different kinds of predictive error can have unequal scientific and
        practical consequences. My research therefore asks whether the use of
        machine learning makes scientific modelling more objective or whether it
        instead transforms, preserves, and displaces the value-laden commitments
        already present in traditional modelling practices.
      </p>
    </div>

  </article>

